lp people  uh we build prosperity but it's also the  best way that everybody can live their  lives according to how they want to live  their lives that's really interesting  and you're right liberal conservatism  isn't uh isn't worth sort of a lot of  politics is anymore um you know the  conservative party in the last few years  has shifted towards sort of more of a  nationalism is that something you're  uncomfortable with  um it's something that i am uh you know  i i'm happy to be in a coalition with  um but you know i was in a coalition  with the lib dems for five years at one  point i was both vince cable's number  two and ed davies number two so i was  kind of there um  working with the working with the lib  dems uh i'm it's i'm more comfortable  with being in coalition with the rest of  the conservative party but all parties  are  coalitions i think that we need to be  clearer and stronger about making the  case for a for a for an economically and  socially liberal conservatism i think  that is actually what where that's where  you know where  that that is where the country is is  strong frankly where we're  forward-looking and  active about embracing modernity um both  on it you know across the board on  economic grounds and on on social  questions and if you're part of a  coalition and some of the party you know  is clearly going into a direction that  you're not entirely comfortable with how  much do you say that how much when  you're around the cabinet table are you  actually making a case for  policies that aren't being followed well  you know do you say this to boris  johnson well i've just said it to you um  yeah of course i make the um  of course i make that i make that  argument that's kind of what i'm you  know that's that's that's what i do and  so around there are these debates around  the cabinet table there's debates in  parliament all of the time and you know  so  uh this debate  on monday we're having a debate on the  use of  um  of gm crops rig